# plans/plan_dx9.md D9-A5 scene corpus: lit_textured_quad_pixellighting
# First scene to exercise BasicEffect.PreferPerPixelLighting=true (plans/plan_dx9.md D9-81 item 1,
# resolved 2026-07-16 -- GpuDrawParams now carries a real preferPerPixelLighting field, and this
# renderer's dispatch selects BasicEffect.fx's own VSBasicPixelLightingTx/PSBasicPixelLightingTx --
# one of the 5 shader variants D9-73 found do NOT byte-match Microsoft's shipped .fxb (a compiler-
# version difference, not a flag), so this scene is also D9-73's own outstanding oracle-proof
# obligation for that specific variant.
#
# A flat, single-normal quad would NOT discriminate this bucket at all: per-vertex lighting
# (Gouraud-interpolating an already-clamped, already-lit per-vertex color) and per-pixel lighting
# (interpolating the raw normal, then computing lit color per pixel) produce IDENTICAL output when
# every vertex shares one normal -- dot(light,N) is linear in a CONSTANT N, so there is nothing to
# interpolate differently. A scene built that way would "pass" even if this renderer's dispatch had
# a bug that silently kept selecting the vertex-lit shader regardless of the flag -- exactly the
# kind of degenerate, proves-nothing scene this corpus has hit before (see fog_gradient_quad.scene's
# own 1st draft, envmap_quad.scene's own accidental-fresnel finding).
#
# So this scene deliberately gives the quad's LEFT-edge vertices a normal facing the light
# (0,0,1), and its RIGHT-edge vertices a normal tilted away from it (0.7071,0,-0.7071,
# dot(-lightDir,N) = -0.7071, NEGATIVE -- clamped to zero by BasicEffect's own max(0,dot) lighting
# math), so BOTH triangles have genuine within-triangle normal variation. Per-vertex lighting
# Gouraud-interpolates the two ALREADY-CLAMPED per-vertex colors (lit white on the left down to
# clamped-zero on the right) -- a LINEAR ramp. Per-pixel lighting instead interpolates the RAW
# normal vector across each triangle, renormalizes it, and clamps AFTER the dot product -- a
# differently-shaped (non-linear, differently-positioned clamp boundary) falloff. The two methods
# are confirmed (empirically, via a scratch, uncommitted vertex-lit twin of this exact scene,
# rendered through the real XNA oracle and diffed against this scene's own real-XNA render) to
# produce genuinely different pixels -- this is not a hand-waved claim.
#
# Solid white 1x1 texture and DiffuseColor left at its BasicEffect default (1,1,1) so the final
# color is exactly the light contribution itself, with no texture/tint interaction to disentangle.
width=256
height=256
profile=HiDef
clearcolor=100,149,237,255
vertexformat=PositionNormalTexture
vertexcolor=false
lighting=true
preferpixellighting=true
texture=true
texturewidth=1
textureheight=1
texturefilter=Point
ambientcolor=0,0,0
light0enabled=true
light0diffuse=0.8,0.8,0.8
light0direction=0,0,-1
primitive=TriangleList
texturepixel=255,255,255,255
# x,y,z,nx,ny,nz,u,v -- left-edge vertices face the light (0,0,1); right-edge vertices tilt away
# (0.7071,0,-0.7071), genuinely varying WITHIN each of the 2 triangles below.
vertex=-0.6,0.6,0,0,0,1,0,0
vertex=0.6,0.6,0,0.7071,0,-0.7071,1,0
vertex=-0.6,-0.6,0,0,0,1,0,1
vertex=0.6,0.6,0,0.7071,0,-0.7071,1,0
vertex=0.6,-0.6,0,0.7071,0,-0.7071,1,1
vertex=-0.6,-0.6,0,0,0,1,0,1
